I think a lot, and about everything, but I'm neither poetic nor a writer. Expressing my thoughts
carefully in words is something I never imagined could be so relieving. I'm afraid this will be my
first and last piece of non-technical writing, though.

I hate Fridays. They're quiet. The bad type of quiet. It's the same feeling as when everyone is
having fun outside and I'm sitting in class because the teacher wanted to punish me. I was the
smartest guy in school, and I never bothered to study until the fifth grade. My throat hurt a lot,
and I could barely breathe. I needed surgery to remove my infected tonsils. It took some time for
me to recover.

My third-grade teacher was a kind old woman. She was always so proud of me. She assigned me an
essay. I know what an essay is. I don't need her explanation. She reads it and looks at me like
I've disappointed her. What? This is impossible. Okay, fine, I'll listen to her and do it again
correctly this time. Again, she isn't satisfied. I don't remember why, but I try a few more times.
The expression on her face is... new! I've failed multiple times, but this can't be my fault.
She's wrong, not me! They told me I'm the smartest. Fuck you and all my classmates. You're all
jealous of my genius. I throw my notebook right into her face and leave the classroom.

A very brave move. Three steps later, I burst into tears. I've cried over much less. Not a big deal.
I don't remember what happened afterward. I probably went home and cried some more.
I no longer throw notebooks at people. I've gotten better at hiding my anger and sadness because no
one has seen me expressing them for years. At this point I think I can't even show them even if I wanted to.

I never open the curtains. The sunlight hitting my monitor is irritating.
"Daylight makes me feel like Dracula." This Friday is different, though. I shall feel the warmth
of the sunlight and the autumn breeze on my face, in the desperate hope of making this a memorable
day for my forgetful brain.

I look for the blue marker, but I give up instantly because I remember my dear cat has left it
somewhere no human, not even one with wrists as small as mine, can reach. That's fine. I bought a
spare black marker for times like this.

A to-do list is a very helpful tool for me. Until I forget I've written one.

[]($image.asset("whiteboard.jpg"))

I like giving people gifts. It's the simplest way to express my interest in and love for them.
Not everyone is grateful, though.

She really liked my T-shirt last night. I was hesitant to give her the shop address because she
might think I'm too stingy to give her mine.

As a kid, I used to lie a lot as a result of having a strict parent. But only I get to decide who
I want to be. Not them. So I send her the address and immediately start messaging every clothing
shop I know, trying to buy a new one. But they all refuse to print it in less than a week, let
alone ship it on a Friday.

Okay, I've got no choice but to give away mine. It's already been washed, but the dust sitting on
it is reason enough to wash it again. I hope she can smell the laundry detergent on it.

I'm all set and ready. I arrive at the bus station, and of course the bus is late! I stand there
for fifteen minutes. I'd predicted these unlucky moments, but I'm starting to get worried.
I won't make it in time without running once I get out of the metro station.

Should I buy a bottle of water? I'll be wasting thirty seconds, but it should be worth it.
I don't wanna pass out from thirst in front of her.

I arrive three minutes late, but she isn't here. What if she's left? No, that can't be it.
I message her because she might not like being called. I wait a few minutes, my heart beating
faster than ever.

Ding! She messages back. Thank God (by God, I mean black tea, of course).

Okay, I'll wait and relax with the heaviest music I can find on this messy playlist. The man in
that shop is looking at me weirdly. Fair enough. I've been pacing back and forth along this
four-meter stretch for fifteen minutes.
I hear a soft female voice behind me. She must be very close because my noise-canceling headphones
barely let any sound through.

I turn around, and yes, it's her!
She's so gorgeous with that big smile. I almost want to say, "Are you an angel?" She'd definitely
get the reference, but no, this is too much. She also looks a bit different from her profile
picture, but to be fair, she looks like a different person in every image I've seen.

I play it cool. I always do. I've learned to stay silent unless I'm spoken to because my thoughts
sound like a stream of nonsense to others. They look at me like I've said something disgusting or
cringe. Or the opposite, if they need my work or money. Will she look at me like that too?

She immediately tries to break the awkward silence. I have a gazillion thoughts in my head, but
I guess it's better to be awkward than to look like an idiot. What should I do? This isn't good.
Isn't adrenaline made for moments like these? Time to lock in. Just talk about the things you
talked about when you were chatting. You two have so many shared interests.

Alright, we reach the coffee shop, and I see her face again.
Those eyes, that nose, that hair. Everything about her is perfect. I wanna stare at her and
appreciate her beauty. Luckily, I'm really bad at maintaining eye contact, so I make it through
safely.

Time passes. She's being so polite and kind. I interrupt her multiple times. I wanna bite the
table and bang my head against the wall. Blaming ADHD but hey! I may just really be stupid.
I should've taken my pills a week earlier, but how could I have known?

Wait, who is this woman? Why is she looking at us like that? Hmm, they're apparently a couple.
They invite me to TehLUG.
I know them. The largest community of proud amateur linux users in Iran. I accept their invitation
because I just wanna end this conversation. They've interrupted us and stolen our precious time.
I'm never going there. Please fuck off. Why do they keep talking?
I look at Raha. She's sitting apart from the conversation and doesn't seem comfortable.
I could've yelled at them to leave us alone, but I think shaking my head was a clear enough
signal that I'm busy and not interested in continuing this conversation.

I already knew she was a smoker. I'd gone through this scenario in my head multiple times.
I don't know how to smoke. Like, come on, it's not rocket science. Everyone is smoking these days.
I always cough and regret it immediately, but she might laugh at me, so why not? Let's cough.
And wow, she offers me her milk. That adds another car to my never-ending train of thoughts.

Apparently, being blessed with myopia wasn't enough. My ears aren't functioning properly either.
She mentions the library on the floor above, but I don't catch it the first time. This happens
multiple times throughout the day.

I like books, but I haven't read many. I abandon them halfway through, just like my projects.
She, on the other hand, has a big bookshelf. I admire knowledgeable people and artists.
She's not an artist, as far as I know, but she knows art. Her taste in music is as beautiful
as her attitude.

We then walk a short distance and reach a park. She wants to sit there, but why?
Does she actually enjoy my company?
This place is too dark. I probably wouldn't walk into a place like this with a guy I'd just met.
Unless she really feels safe with me?

We talk about personal stuff. Life, family, and music. We listen to songs together. I don't
listen to much Farsi music. I've always thought there weren't many good tracks.
But she proves me wrong. She is mad that I didn't notice her hair color changed. I did but not today..
I wish I could see the world from her lens. It must be different. Smart people can separate facts and emotions.
She is very capable of that. I can't control it. Emotions never obeyed me. Why?

She lights my cigarette. I still don't know how to smoke, but I take gentle puffs to make sure
I won't cough again. I experience an extraordinary feeling. I wish it lasted longer.
Being next to her is now all I can think about.

The ending is no less pleasant. We walk toward the metro and wait there for a while.
I suddenly remember the T-shirt. She looks so happy about it. I'm bursting with joy,
but as always, I probably forget to show it on my face. I even give her that calm,
analytical stare when she says it fits her!!
Like, how hard could it possibly be for you to just tell her it fits her??
She compliments my necklace, just as she'd complimented my hair and nails twice.
Is that a sign? Does she like me? Yes? No, shut up. Don't overthink it.
She is just being a nice friend. Ok?

She shows me her necklace. It's as cute as she looks leaning against the train door.
She even offers to make one for me. A gift made for me by her little hands? I'm never taking that off.
I can look at it during the long blue nights and say good night to it!

We wave goodbye and go our separate ways. It's a sad and happy moment.
I know I could fly if the metro ceiling didn't block me.
But hold on. I realize I've left her to make her way back to the dormitory alone.
At 9 p.m.!! What an idiot I am. Luckily, she messages back and says she got there safely.

Well, that was the happiest day of my twenty years of life. I can finally collapse into bed.

The next day, I spread out the stack of books she chose for me.
It's easy to pick the first one to read. *The Little Prince* is the shortest and the easiest to read.
Breakfast is my favorite meal, but I put it off today because I can't stop reading the book and
wondering how she might have felt about every line.

I smoke a light cigarette while writing this. Alas it's not the same as the one she offered.
It may look ironic that I'm now entering a path to destroy my lungs while also putting such
hard effort in climbing and cycling. But I'm not doing any of that to get healthy or look buff.
I really just enjoy them. Climbing makes it easier to imagine myself as Spider-Man.
